  "narrative": "No assessments are due and the independent-validation budget is exhausted, so this explore step registers only discovery-only comparisons. These are new falsifiable effect-modification and high-quality robustness tests, not reversals of existing claims, so no parent links are used. All controller estimates will already be signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 mean or interaction claims, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency in the exposed contrast because the underlying CRISPR dependency score is expected to be more negative. For direction +1 claims, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency in the exposed contrast. For interactions, a positive signed estimate means the exposed-minus-comparator contrast is stronger in the named subgroup than in its complement within the same eligible population. I will interpret intervals crossing zero as unresolved rather than evidence of absence, while using the approximate 0.025 absolute dependency-score practical threshold for main effects and being cautious with interaction magnitudes.\n\nThe first proposal tests whether the accepted CDKN2A-loss stronger MDM2-dependency association is modified by TP53-loss context. The parent MDM2 effect is robust overall and in both TP53-loss and TP53-intact contexts, so the primary anticipated result is null effect modification; a clearly positive interaction would suggest TP53-loss amplifies the CDKN2A-MDM2 association, while a clearly negative interaction would suggest attenuation in TP53-loss lines. The second MDM2 proposal tests the biologically related 9p21 co-deletion context using MTAP loss as the subgroup. Because the MTAP-loss co-deletion context has been less mature than the broad CDKN2A effect, a positive interaction would support context-specific amplification in MTAP-loss lines, whereas null or imprecise intervals would leave co-deletion-specific interpretation uncertain.\n\nThe STAT3 proposal tests whether the accepted PTEN-loss stronger STAT3-dependency association is modified by screen batch. The broad association is strong and validated, so the expected result is null batch effect modification. A large batch-specific interaction would be useful because it would indicate that part of the measured magnitude may be technical or correlated-screen context, although it would not by itself establish causality. The DHX9 proposal tests whether the modest accepted RB1-loss weaker DHX9-dependency association is modified by TP53-loss context. The core effect is positive but platform-limited, so null or attenuation is plausible; a clear interaction would indicate co-alteration context that deserves careful scrutiny.\n\nThe USP7 proposal tests whether the very strong TP53-no-loss stronger USP7-dependency association is modified by KEAP1-loss context. The broad TP53-USP7 association is highly robust, so the expected result is null effect modification. A precisely estimated non-null interaction would be informative for NFE2L2/oxidative-stress pathway context, but a null result should not be overinterpreted. The SMARCA2 proposal tests whether the very large SMARCA4-loss stronger SMARCA2-dependency association is modified by PIK3CA mutation. Null effect modification is expected because the parent association is robust across many contexts; a strong interaction would identify a plausible pathway-specific modifier.\n\nThe NFE2L2 proposal tests whether KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2-dependency is modified by CDKN2A loss. Because the KEAP1-NFE2L2 effect is large and broad, the primary expectation is null; any interaction could reflect co-occurring cell-cycle or chromosomal context. The WRN proposal tests whether the highly reproducible MSI-high stronger WRN-dependency association is modified by TP53-loss context. The TP53-loss main effect was slightly larger than the overall MSI effect, but overlap and multiplicity make null the prudent expectation; a clear interaction would be scientifically useful but still associational.\n\nThe final proposal tests TP53-loss versus TP53-intact KIF18A dependency within AVANA-library screens with screen ROC at least 0.95 and Cas9 activity score at least 0.85. The broad positive signal was near the practical threshold and higher-quality subsets remained uncertain. This high-quality stratum tests whether a TP53-loss KIF18A association becomes clearer after reducing Cas9 and screen-quality noise. The anticipated result is unresolved: a small true effect is plausible, but a trivial platform-associated signal is also plausible. The Cas9 threshold was chosen at 0.85 to retain usable exposed cell counts while still excluding low-editing screens. These comparisons are supplied-dataset associations only and do not establish causality, therapeutic vulnerability, or external generalizability."
